"Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds (https://arxiv.org/html/2211.15970)\nciteturn2view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view0\",\"pattern\":\"hyperplane\"}); Total lines: 3025\nL437: Applying cite13†3.2 with $J=I_{st}$ and with the hyperplane $\\Sigma={\\mathbb{C}}P^{n-1}\\subset({\\mathbb{C}}P^{n},I_{st})$, we get the following corollary.\nL438: \nL439: Corollary 3.11\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds (https://arxiv.org/html/2211.15970)\nciteturn2view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view0\",\"pattern\":\"Theorem 3.4\"}); Total lines: 3025\nL77: There do exist symplectic embeddings of balls that are not of Kähler type (see below). We do not know whether these symplectic embeddings are tame or whether there exist tame embeddings (of balls or any other domains) that are not of Kähler-type – see cite15†3.4 and cite15†3.4 .\nL358: For rational or ruled closed symplectic manifolds of real dimension $4$ we have a stronger connectedness result, which follows directly from similar connectedness results for symplectic (not necessarily Kähler-type) embeddings of balls that were proved in [cite42†McD3 ] and [cite48†BLW ] (for previous partial results see [cite43†McD1 , cite44†McD2 , cite45†Lal , cite46†Bir1 ]).\nL359: \nL360: Theorem 3.4\nL384: ### 3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products\nL385: \nL386: Denote by $\\omega_{FS}$, or by $\\omega_{FS,n}$, the Fubini-Study form on ${\\mathbb{C}}P^{n}$ normalized so that $\\int_{{\\mathbb{C}}P^{1}}\\omega_{FS}=\\pi$. Let $I_{st}$ be the standard complex structure on ${\\mathbb{C}}P^{n}$.\nL387: \nL388: We also set ${\\mathbb{C}}P^{0}:=\\textrm{pt}$.\nL389: \nL390: Theorem 3.5\nL483: B. Symplectic embeddings of a disjoint union of $k=l^{2}$, $l\\in{\\mathbb{Z}}_{>0}$, equal balls into $({\\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\\setminus\\Sigma,\\omega_{FS})$ are unobstructed: if $k{\\rm{Vol}\\,}(B^{4}(r),\\omega_{0})<{\\rm{Vol}\\,}({\\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\\omega_{FS})$, then there exists a symplectic embedding $\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r)\\to({\\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\\setminus\\Sigma,\\omega_{FS})$.\nL484: \nL485: Here is an extension of cite13†3.2 to Kähler-type embeddings of balls into ${\\mathbb{C}}P^{2}$.\nL486: \nL487: Theorem 3.15\nL588: ### 3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces\nL589: \nL590: Let $M$, $\\dim_{\\mathbb{R}}M=2n$, be either ${\\mathbb{T}}^{2n}$ or a smooth manifold (of real dimension $4$) underlying a complex K3 surface.\nL591: \nL592: As before, let $\\omega$ be a Kähler-type symplectic form on $M$.\nL593: \nL594: In the case $M={\\mathbb{T}}^{2n}$ one can assume, without loss of generality, that $\\omega$ is linear (see cite8†2.1 ).\nL598: In fact, a Kähler-type complex structure on a torus is Campana-simple if and only if it has no proper positive-dimensional complex subvarieties (see cite30†10.1 ). A symplectic form $\\omega$ on the torus or a K3 surface is compatible with such a complex structure if and only if $\\omega$ is irrational (see cite30†10.1 and cite31†10.2 ). See Section cite16†3.5 for other examples of manifolds admitting Campana-simple complex structures.\nL599: \nL600: Theorem 3.22\nL619: In particular, for any symplectic form $\\omega$ on ${\\mathbb{T}}^{2}$ (note that any such form is rational!) the result of part A of cite15†3.4 is true and the space of Kähler-type embeddings of any disjoint union of domains into $({\\mathbb{T}}^{2},\\omega)$ is path-connected (since the space of symplectic forms on ${\\mathbb{T}}^{2}$ is path-connected [cite91†Mos ]).\nL620: For the proof of cite15†3.4 see Section cite32†10.3 (cite32†10.3 ).\nL621: \nL622: For a generalization of cite15†3.4 to arbitrary closed symplectic manifolds admitting Campana-simple complex structures compatible with the symplectic form see cite28†9 . In fact, we will use cite28†9 in the proof of cite15†3.4 .\nL623: \nL624: For tori the existence result in cite15†3.4 admits the following equivalent formulation.\nL625: \nL626: Corollary 3.24\nL627: Assume $\\Gamma\\subset{\\mathbb{C}}^{n}$ is a lattice of rank $2n$. Let $M:={\\mathbb{C}}^{n}/\\Gamma$ be the corresponding torus. Let $\\omega,I$ be the symplectic and the complex structures on $M$ induced by the standard flat symplectic and complex structures on ${\\mathbb{C}}^{n}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2n}$.\nL628: Assume that $(M,I)$ admits no proper positive-dimensional complex subvarieties. Let $k\\in{\\mathbb{Z}}_{>0}$, $r_{1},\\ldots,r_{k}>0$ such that ${\\rm{Vol}\\,}\\left(\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i}),\\omega_{0}\\right)<{\\rm{Vol}\\,}(M,\\omega)$.\nL629: Then there exists a Kähler form $\\omega^{\\prime}$ on $(M,I)$ isotopic to $\\omega$ and a Kähler-type embedding $\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\\to(M,\\omega^{\\prime})$ which is holomorphic with respect to $I$ – i.e., an embedding which is symplectic with respect to $\\omega^{\\prime}$ and holomorphic with respect to $I$.\nL630: \nL631: Proof of cite15†3.4 :\nL632: It suffices to prove a similar claim for the case where $\\Gamma={\\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}$ and $\\omega$ and $I$ are compatible symplectic and complex structures on ${\\mathbb{T}}^{2n}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2n}/{\\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}$, with $I$ being Campana-simple (the proof can be reduced to this case by using a diffeomorphism $M={\\mathbb{C}}^{n}/\\Gamma\\to{\\mathbb{T}}^{2n}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2n}/{\\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}$ induced by an ${\\mathbb{R}}$-linear isomorphism of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2n}={\\mathbb{C}}^{2n}$ sending $\\Gamma$ to ${\\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}$).\nL633: In order to prove the corollary in this case, apply part A of cite15†3.4 and keep the complex structure fixed while changing the symplectic form by an isotopy (cf. cite10†2.3 ).\nL634: Remark 3.25\nL635: \nL636: 1. For an arbitrary Kähler-type symplectic form $\\omega$ on $M={\\mathbb{T}}^{2n}$ or on a manifold $M$ underlying a K3 surface, the unobstructedness of symplectic – but not necessarily Kähler-type or tame – embeddings of an arbitrary collection of disjoint balls was proved in [cite56†EV1 , Thm. 3.1]. In the case $M={\\mathbb{T}}^{4}$ a weaker version of this result had been previously proved by Latschev-McDuff-Schlenk [cite92†LatMS ].\nL637: 2. It is a well-known open problem (see e.g. [cite78†McDS , Ch.15, Prob. 15]) whether $\\mathrm{Symp}_{H}({\\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\\omega)=\\mathrm{Symp}_{0}({\\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\\omega)$ for a Kähler-type symplectic form $\\omega$ on ${\\mathbb{T}}^{2n}$, $n>1$.\nL638: If the answer to this question is positive, then Part B of cite15†3.4 would imply that the space of the Kähler-type embeddings $\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\\to({\\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\\omega)$ is path-connected for any $k\\in{\\mathbb{Z}}_{>0}$ and $r_{1},\\ldots,r_{k}>0$. Conversely, a counter-example to the latter claim would show that $\\mathrm{Symp}_{H}({\\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\\omega)\\neq\\mathrm{Symp}_{0}({\\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\\omega)$.\nL639: In the case when $M$ is a manifold underlying a K3 surface it is known by the result of Sheridan-Smith [cite93†ShS ] that $\\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\\omega)\\neq\\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\\omega)$ for certain Kähler-type symplectic structures $\\omega$ on $M$, including some irrational ones.\nL640: It has been known since the work [cite94†Sei ] of Seidel that for some (not necessarily irrational) Kähler-type symplectic forms $\\omega$ on $M$ one has $\\mathrm{Symp}(M,\\omega)\\cap\\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)\\neq\\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\\omega)$; by a result of Smirnov [cite95†Sm ], this definitely holds for some irrational Kähler-type symplectic forms $\\omega$ on $M$.\nL641: 3. cite15†3.4 for one ellipsoid – and, in particular, for one ball – was previously proved by Luef and Wang [cite52†LuWa ] using a method similar to the one in this paper. Let us note that the paper [cite52†LuWa ] by Luef and Wang provides a very interesting connection between Kähler-type embeddings of ellipsoids into tori and Gabor frames – an important notion in signal processing.\nL642: We expect that the technique used in [cite52†LuWa ], as well as the techniques developed in [cite96†EV2 ] in relation to symplectic embeddings of ellipsoids into tori and K3 surfaces, can be used to generalize cite15†3.4 to Kähler-type embeddings of ellipsoids.\nL643: \nL644: Remark 3.26\nL645: The assumption that the symplectic form $\\omega$ on $M$ is irrational is essential in cite15†3.4 : otherwise there may be obstructions for the existence of Kähler-type embeddings of (disjoint unions of) balls into $(M,\\omega)$ that are independent of the symplectic volume – for instance, the obstructions coming from Seshadri constants.\nL646: \nL647: For simplicity, we will explain this below in the case of one ball and $M={\\mathbb{T}}^{2n}$, expanding on [cite92†LatMS , Sec. 2.2].\nL648: First, recall the definition of Seshadri constants. Let $(X,I)$ be a complex projective manifold and let $\\omega$ be a Kähler form on $(X,I)$ such that the cohomology class $[\\omega]$ is integral and equals the first Chern class of an ample line bundle on $(X,I)$. Assume that $x\\in X$, $\\pi:{\\widetilde{X}}\\to X$ is the complex blow-up of $X$ at $x$, and $E:=\\pi^{-1}(x)$ is the exceptional divisor. Define the Seshadri constant\nL649:  | $$\\epsilon(X,I,[\\omega],x):=\\sup\\left\\{s>0\\ \\left|\\right.\\ \\left\\langle\\pi^{*}[\\omega],[{\\widetilde{C}}]\\right\\rangle-sE\\cdot[{\\widetilde{C}}]\\geq 0\\right.$$  |\nL650:  | $$\\left.\\textrm{for all (possibly singular) complex curves}\\ {\\widetilde{C}}\\subset{\\widetilde{X}}\\ \\right\\}.$$  |\nL651: \nL652: Observe that $E\\cdot[{\\widetilde{C}}]$ is the multiplicity of the singularity of $C$ at $x$.\nL653: \nL654: Then\nL676: Let us now consider tame embeddings of balls into tori and K3 surfaces.\nL677: \nL678: Theorem 3.27\nL679: \nL680: Let $M$, $\\dim_{\\mathbb{R}}M=2n$, be either ${\\mathbb{T}}^{2n}$ or a smooth manifold (of real dimension $4$) underlying a complex K3 surface.\nL681: \nL682: Let $\\omega_{1}$, $\\omega_{2}$ be Kähler-type forms on $M$ that are irrational and satisfy $\\int_{M}\\omega_{1}^{n}=\\int_{M}\\omega_{2}^{n}>0$.\nL683: Let $W_{i}\\subset{\\mathbb{R}}^{2n}$, $i=1,\\ldots,k$, be compact domains with piecewise-smooth boundary whose interiors contain the origin. Assume that $H^{2}(W_{i};{\\mathbb{R}})=0$ for all $i$. Set ${\\mathbb{W}}:=\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}W_{i}$.\nL684: \nL685: Then, for any $\\varepsilon>0$,\nL686: \nL687:  | $$\\nu_{T,\\varepsilon}(M,\\omega_{1},{\\mathbb{W}})=\\nu_{T,\\varepsilon}(M,\\omega_{2},{\\mathbb{W}}).$$  |\nL688: \nL689: For the proof of cite15†3.4 see Section cite33†11 (cite33†11 ).\nL690: \nL691: Remark 3.28\nL692: cite15†3.4 generalizes a similar result in [cite56†EV1 ] about symplectic (not necessarily tame) embeddings and its proof is similar to the proof in [cite56†EV1 ].\nL693: \nL694: Let us present applications of cite15†3.4 .\nL695: \nL696: Corollary 3.29\nL697: \nL698: Assume:\nL699: \nL700:   * •\nL701: \nL702: $M$ is either ${\\mathbb{T}}^{2n}$ or a smooth manifold underlying a K3 surface.\nL703: \nL704:   * •\nL705: \nL706: ${\\mathbb{W}}:=\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})$ is a disjoint union of $k$ (possibly different) balls.\nL707: \nL708:   * •\nL709: \nL710: $\\varepsilon>0$.\nL711: \nL712: Then the following claims hold:\nL765: Then there exists a $\\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)$-invariant open dense set of Kähler-type symplectic forms on $M$, depending on ${\\mathbb{W}}$ and $\\varepsilon$ and containing, in particular, all irrational Kähler-type symplectic forms on $M$, so that for each $\\omega^{\\prime}$ in this set $\\nu_{T,\\varepsilon}(M,\\omega^{\\prime},{\\mathbb{W}})=1$, meaning that $\\varepsilon$-tame embeddings of $\\lambda{\\mathbb{W}}$ into $(M,\\omega^{\\prime})$ are unobstructed.\nL766: For the proof of cite15†3.4 , cite15†3.4 and cite15†3.4 see Section cite33†11 . These corollaries generalize similar results for symplectic – but not necessarily tame – embeddings that were proved in [cite56†EV1 ].\nL767: \nL768: Remark 3.32\nL769: We know only one way to show the possible existence of embeddings (of a disjoint union of domains) into $(M,\\omega)$ that are symplectic and non-tame, or tame and not Kähler-type – via the volume restrictions: e.g., if Kähler-type embeddings cannot fill more than a certain portion of the volume of $(M,\\omega)$ while tame embeddings can. This is how we got in cite15†3.4 that there exist symplectic embeddings (of balls) that are not of Kähler-type.\nL770: It would be interesting to find out whether there are other ways of detecting symplectic and non-tame, or tame and not Kähler-type, embeddings.\nL771: Together with cite15†3.4 , this leads to the following questions.\nL772: \nL773: Question 3.33\nL774: Does there exist a closed manifold equipped with a Kähler-type symplectic form that admits a symplectic embedding which is not tame, or a tame embedding which is not of Kähler-type? In particular, does there exist a rational Kähler-type symplectic form $\\omega$ on $M={\\mathbb{T}}^{2n}$, $n>1$, or on a smooth manifold $M$ underlying a complex K3 surface, and a disjoint union of balls ${\\mathbb{W}}$, ${\\rm{Vol}\\,}({\\mathbb{W}},\\omega_{0})<{\\rm{Vol}\\,}(M,\\omega)$, so that ${\\mathbb{W}}$ does not admit a tame embedding into $(M,\\omega)$?\nL775: ### 3.5 The IHS-hyperkähler case\nL776: \nL777: K3 surfaces are particular examples of so called IHS-hyperkähler manifolds, that is, compact hyperkähler manifolds of maximal holonomy. Most of the results of Section cite15†3.4 generalize to such manifolds. In this section we present relevant definitions and statements.\nL778: Recall that a hyperkähler manifold is a manifold equipped with three complex structures $I_{1},I_{2},I_{3}$ satisfying the quaternionic relations and three symplectic forms $\\omega_{1},\\omega_{2},\\omega_{3}$ compatible, respectively, with $I_{1},I_{2},I_{3}$, so that the three Riemannian metrics $\\omega_{i}(\\cdot,I_{i}\\cdot)$, $i=1,2,3$, coincide.\nL797: Note that, in view of part 2 of cite16†3.5 , for symplectic embeddings into a K3 surface being of IHS-hyperkähler type is the same as being of Kähler type.\nL798: \nL799: The results of Section cite15†3.4 on Kähler-type embeddings generalize to the IHS-hyperkähler case as follows:\nL800: \nL801: - Part A of cite15†3.4 generalizes directly to the IHS-hyperkähler case as long as one assumes that the Campana-simple complex structure $I$ is of IHS-hyperkähler-type.\nL802: - The claim in part C of cite15†3.4 concerning the action of $\\mathrm{Symp}(M,\\omega)\\cap\\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)$ generalizes to the IHS-hyperkähler case, if $\\mathit{CK}(M)$ is replaced by the space of the IHS-hyperkähler-type complex structures on $M$. For a general IHS-hyperkähler manifold $(M,\\omega)$ we cannot say anything about the transitivity of the $\\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\\omega)$-action.\nL803: Let us discuss the results on $\\varepsilon$-tame embeddings into $(M,\\omega)$ for an IHS-hyperkähler-type form $\\omega$. One can define tame and $\\varepsilon$-tame embeddings into $(M,\\omega)$ exactly as in cite10†2.3 using Kähler-type complex structures. Another possibility is to modify the definition of tame and $\\varepsilon$-tame embeddings into $(M,\\omega)$ by using IHS-hyperkähler-type complex structures in cite10†2.3 instead of Kähler-type ones.\nL804: The claim $\\nu_{T,\\varepsilon}(M,\\omega_{1},{\\mathbb{W}})=\\nu_{T,\\varepsilon}(M,\\omega_{2},{\\mathbb{W}})$ of cite15†3.4 holds for the original $\\nu_{T,\\varepsilon}$ defined in cite10†2.3 , as well as for a version of $\\nu_{T,\\varepsilon}$ defined using the $\\varepsilon$-tame embeddings in the second sense above, if $\\omega_{1}$ and $\\omega_{2}$ lie in the same connected component of the space of IHS-hyperkähler-type symplectic forms on $M$.\nL805: In Section cite34†12 we discuss how to modify the proofs of the results of Section cite15†3.4 in order to obtain the above-mentioned results in the IHS-hyperkähler case.\nL806: ## 4 Plan of the paper and an outline of the proofs\nL807: \nL808: In this section we describe the plan of the paper and outline the proofs.\nL809: \nL810: In order to simplify the exposition, let us fix a connected component ${\\mathcal{C}}_{0}$ of $\\mathit{CK}(M)$ and the radii $r_{1},\\ldots,r_{k}>0$ of the balls from the beginning. Let ${\\bar{r}}:=(r_{1},\\ldots,r_{k})$.\nL811: \nL812: In Section cite18†5 we prove cite12†3.1 and cite28†9 . Namely, we define the following spaces:\nL828: The general results on Kähler-type embeddings of balls into such manifolds, proved in Section cite28†9 , are used further in the paper to deduce the results on such embeddings into tori and K3 surfaces stated in Section cite15†3.4 .\nL833: In Section cite33†11 we discuss the proofs of the results in Section cite15†3.4 . concerning tame embeddings of arbitrary domains. The strategy of the proof of cite15†3.4 is the same as for a similar claim in [cite56†EV1 ]. Namely, with $M$ being either ${\\mathbb{T}}^{2n}$ or a smooth manifold underlying a K3 surface, the group $\\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)$ of orientation-preserving diffeomorphisms of $M$ acts on the space $\\mathit{SK}_{1}(M)$ of Kähler-type forms on $M$ of total volume $1$.\nL837: Then the proofs of cite15†3.4 , cite15†3.4 and cite15†3.4 follow the same lines as the proof of [cite56†EV1 , Cor. 3.3] where the existence of symplectic embeddings of polydisks into tori was proved.\nL2197: ### 10.3 Kähler-type embeddings of balls into tori and K3 surfaces – the proofs\nL2198: \nL2199: Now we are ready to prove cite15†3.4 . For convenience we restate it here.\nL2200: \nL2216: C. Any two Kähler-type embeddings $\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\\to(M,\\omega)$ (if they exist!) lie in the same orbit of the $\\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\\omega)$-action. They lie in the same orbit of the $\\mathrm{Symp}(M,\\omega)\\cap\\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)$-action if and only if they favor a common connected component of $\\mathit{CK}(M)$. In the latter case there exists $[I]\\in{\\tt Teich}(M,\\omega)$ such that both embeddings are of $[I]$-Kähler-type.\nL2217: \nL2218: Proof of cite32†10.3 (=cite15†3.4 ):\nL2221: ## 11 Tame embeddings of arbitrary domains\nL2222: \nL2223: In this section we will prove the results on tame embeddings into tori and K3 surfaces stated in Section cite15†3.4 .\nL2224: \nL2225: Let $M$, $\\dim_{\\mathbb{R}}M=2n$, be a closed connected oriented manifold admitting Kähler structures (compatible with the orientation).\nL2312:  | $$\\frac{{\\rm{Vol}\\,}(\\lambda{\\mathbb{W}},\\omega_{0})}{{\\rm{Vol}\\,}(M,\\omega^{\\prime})}>\\frac{{\\rm{Vol}\\,}(\\lambda{\\mathbb{W}},\\omega_{0})}{{\\rm{Vol}\\,}(M,\\omega)}-\\delta,\\ \\forall\\omega^{\\prime}\\in{\\mathcal{U}}.$$  |\nL2313: \nL2314: This shows that $\\nu_{T,\\varepsilon}$ is lower semicontinuous at $\\omega$.\nL2315: \nL2316: Thus, $\\nu_{T,\\varepsilon}$ is lower semicontinuous at all $\\omega\\in\\mathit{S}(M)$, meaning that it is a lower semicontinuous function.\nL2317: \nL2318: Let us now prove cite15†3.4 . For convenience, we restate it here.\nL2319: Theorem 11.6 (= cite15†3.4 )\nL2320: \nL2321: Let $M$ be either a torus ${\\mathbb{T}}^{2n}$ or a smooth manifold underlying a K3 surface.\nL2322: \nL2323: Let $\\omega_{1}$, $\\omega_{2}$ be Kähler-type forms on $M$ so that $\\int_{M}\\omega_{1}^{n}=\\int_{M}\\omega_{2}^{n}>0$ and the forms $\\omega_{1}$, $\\omega_{2}$ are irrational.\nL2324: Let $W_{i}\\subset{\\mathbb{R}}^{2n}$, $i=1,\\ldots,k$, be compact domains with piecewise-smooth boundary whose interiors contain the origin. Assume that $H^{2}(W_{i};{\\mathbb{R}})=0$ for all $i$. Set ${\\mathbb{W}}:=\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}W_{i}$.\nL2325: \nL2326: Then, for any $\\varepsilon>0$,\nL2327: \nL2328:  | $$\\nu_{T,\\varepsilon}(M,\\omega_{1},{\\mathbb{W}})=\\nu_{T,\\varepsilon}(M,\\omega_{2},{\\mathbb{W}}).$$  |\nL2329: \nL2330: Proof of cite33†11 (= cite15†3.4 ):\nL2369: This finishes the proof of part B and of the corollary.\nL2370: \nL2371: Corollary 11.8 (= cite15†3.4 )\nL2372: \nL2373: Assume:\nL2374: \nL2375:   * •\nL2376: \nL2377: $M$ is either ${\\mathbb{T}}^{2n}$ or a smooth manifold underlying a K3 surface.\nL2378: \nL2379:   * •\nL2380: \nL2381: ${\\mathbb{W}}:=\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})$ is a disjoint union of $k$ (possibly different) balls.\nL2382: \nL2383:   * •\nL2384: \nL2385: $\\varepsilon>0$.\nL2386: \nL2387: Then the following claims hold:\nL2388: \nL2389: A. For any irrational Kähler-type symplectic form $\\omega$ on $M$ we have\nL2390: \nL2391:  | $$\\nu_{K}(M,\\omega,{\\mathbb{W}})=1,$$  |\nL2393: B. There exists a $\\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)$-invariant open dense set of Kähler-type symplectic forms on $M$, depending on ${\\mathbb{W}}$ and $\\varepsilon$ and containing, in particular, all irrational Kähler-type symplectic forms on $M$, so that for each $\\omega$ in this set $\\nu_{T,\\varepsilon}(M,\\omega,{\\mathbb{W}})=1$ – meaning that $\\varepsilon$-tame embeddings of $\\lambda{\\mathbb{W}}$ into $(M,\\omega)$ are unobstructed.\nL2394: \nL2395: Proof of cite33†11 (=cite15†3.4 ):\nL2396: Assume without loss of generality that $n>1$. (In the case $n=1$ the result is known – see cite15†3.4 ).\nL2397: \nL2398: Part A of the corollary is equivalent to the existence claim proved in cite15†3.4 .\nL2399: \nL2400: Part B of the corollary follows then from part B of cite33†11 .\nL2401: \nL2402: Now let us prove cite15†3.4 . We recall it here.\nL2403: \nL2404: Corollary 11.9 (= cite15†3.4 )\nL2405: \nL2406: Assume:\nL2407: \nL2408:   * •\nL2409: \nL2410: $M={\\mathbb{T}}^{2n}$.\nL2411: \nL2412:   * •\nL2413: \nL2414: ${\\mathbb{W}}:=\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}W_{i}$ is either a disjoint union of $k$ identical copies of a $2n$-dimensional polydisk\nL2431: Proof of cite33†11 (=cite15†3.4 ):\nL2432: \nL2433: Assume without loss of generality that $n>1$. (In the case $n=1$ the result is known – see cite15†3.4 ).\nL2434: \nL2435: Let us consider the case where ${\\mathbb{W}}$ is the disjoint union of $k$ identical copies of the polydisk $B^{2n_{1}}(R_{1})\\times\\ldots\\times B^{2n_{l}}(R_{l})$. In this case the proof is the following straightforward modification of the proof of [cite56†EV1 , Cor. 3.3].\nL2569: Then there exists a $\\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)$-invariant open dense set of Kähler-type symplectic forms on $M$, depending on ${\\mathbb{W}}$ and $\\varepsilon$ and containing, in particular, all irrational Kähler-type symplectic forms on $M$, so that for each $\\omega^{\\prime}$ in this set $\\nu_{T,\\varepsilon}(M,\\omega^{\\prime},{\\mathbb{W}})=1$, meaning that $\\varepsilon$-tame embeddings of $\\lambda{\\mathbb{W}}$ into $(M,\\omega^{\\prime})$ are unobstructed.\nL2570: \nL2571: Proof of cite33†11 (=cite15†3.4 ):\nL2730: This finishes the proof of the proposition.\nL2731: \nL2732: We are now ready to discuss the analogue of cite15†3.4 in the IHS-hyperkähler case, as described in Section cite16†3.5 .\nL2733: The proof of the claims of cite15†3.4 in the IHS-hyperkähler case (apart from the claim on the transitivity of the $\\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\\omega)$-action) is the same as in the K3 case – one just needs to use the generalizations of the results of Section cite31†10.2 to the IHS-hyperkähler case discussed above (instead of the corresponding results for the K3 surfaces) and use cite28†9 in the simply-connected case involving Campana-simple complex structures.\nL2738: The claim $\\nu_{T,\\varepsilon}(M,\\omega_{1},{\\mathbb{W}})=\\nu_{T,\\varepsilon}(M,\\omega_{2},{\\mathbb{W}})$ of cite15†3.4 holds for both definitions of $\\nu_{T,\\varepsilon}$, as long as $\\omega_{1}$ and $\\omega_{2}$ lie in the same connected component of the space of IHS-hyperkähler-type symplectic forms on $M$. Indeed, it is easy to see that cite33†11 holds for both definitions of $\\nu_{T,\\varepsilon}$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds (https://arxiv.org/html/2211.15970)\nciteturn2view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view0\",\"pattern\":\"Theorem 2.15\"}); Total lines: 3025\nNo matching text found for \"Theorem 2.15\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2211.15970] Kahler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds (https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.15970)\nciteturn2view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view2\",\"pattern\":\"[v\"}); Total lines: 166\nNo matching text found for \"[v\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[1112.1149] Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability (https://arxiv.org/abs/1112.1149)\nciteturn2view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view1\",\"pattern\":\"[v\"}); Total lines: 160\nNo matching text found for \"[v\""
